<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anton Denikin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1872–1947</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Anton Ivanovich Denikin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anton Denikin was a Russian general and a leading commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-movement">White movement</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>. As head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armed-forces-of-south-russia">Armed Forces of South Russia</a> he advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1919, taking much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, but was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> near Oryol and driven back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. He resigned his command in 1920 and died in exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anton-denikin.jpg" alt="Anton Denikin" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denikin1_(cropped).PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
